--TEST--
Core\Crypto::open refuses an altered message, a truncated one, a buffer too short to be one and the wrong key with a single RuntimeError, while a key of the wrong length is a LogicError instead — `rule:core-api/tier-roster`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound asserted on both sides. `openssl_decrypt` answers `false` on a
// forgery when it notices one at all, and an unauthenticated mode hands back
// whatever is left of the plaintext; an AEAD returns nothing. The four
// refusals below are four different ways of not being authentic and they all
// print the same sentence, which is the point rather than an economy: telling
// them apart tells a forger which half of the attempt landed.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $other = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
bytes $sealed = Core\Crypto::seal(
    "attack at dawn" as bytes,
    $key,
    Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305
);

// One octet short — the cheapest tamper there is, and `examples/crypto.nvs`'s
// own last line.
bytes $truncated = Core\Bytes::slice($sealed, 0, (Core\Bytes::length($sealed) as int) - 1);
try {
    bytes $forged = Core\Crypto::open($truncated, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    echo "tamper accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The nonce on its own: well-formed as far as the layout goes, and carrying no
// tag at all.
try {
    bytes $headless = Core\Crypto::open(
        Core\Bytes::slice($sealed, 0, 24),
        $key,
        Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305
    );
    echo "a bare nonce accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $noTag) {
    echo $noTag->message, "\n";
}

// Too short to hold a nonce and a tag between them, so there is nothing to
// split — and it is refused with the same sentence rather than a more helpful
// one about its length.
try {
    bytes $stub = Core\Crypto::open("nope" as bytes, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    echo "a four-octet message accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $tooShort) {
    echo $tooShort->message, "\n";
}

// An intact message under a key that did not seal it.
try {
    bytes $wrong = Core\Crypto::open($sealed, $other, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    echo "opened under the wrong key", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notThisKey) {
    echo $notThisKey->message, "\n";
}

// The one failure that is *not* about the message. A `bytes` of the wrong
// width was never a key, so it is a program bug rather than a forgery, and it
// gets a class and a sentence of its own — with the length named and the
// value not.
secret bytes $stunted = Core\Random::bytes(8);
try {
    bytes $unopenable = Core\Crypto::open($sealed, $stunted, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    echo "opened under an 8-octet key", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $notAKey) {
    echo $notAKey->message, "\n";
}

try {
    bytes $unsealable = Core\Crypto::seal(
        "x" as bytes,
        $stunted,
        Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305
    );
    echo "sealed under an 8-octet key", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $stillNotAKey) {
    echo $stillNotAKey->message, "\n";
}

// The other side of the bound: the message this class sealed opens under the
// key that sealed it, so the six refusals above are about their arguments and
// not about `open` having become a member that refuses everything.
if (Core\Crypto::open($sealed, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305) == "attack at dawn" as bytes) {
    echo "and the message it sealed opens", "\n";
} else {
    echo "refused its own message", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Crypto::open(): $sealed is not an authentic message under $key — it has been altered, it is too short to be one, or the key is not the one it was sealed under
Core\Crypto::open(): $sealed is not an authentic message under $key — it has been altered, it is too short to be one, or the key is not the one it was sealed under
Core\Crypto::open(): $sealed is not an authentic message under $key — it has been altered, it is too short to be one, or the key is not the one it was sealed under
Core\Crypto::open(): $sealed is not an authentic message under $key — it has been altered, it is too short to be one, or the key is not the one it was sealed under
Core\Crypto::open(): $key is 8 octets, and a key is 32 — Core\Crypto::generateKey() answers one of the right length. The value is not quoted here, because a key does not belong in a log.
Core\Crypto::seal(): $key is 8 octets, and a key is 32 — Core\Crypto::generateKey() answers one of the right length. The value is not quoted here, because a key does not belong in a log.
and the message it sealed opens
